WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.656
Seven hundred, a thousand, sixteen hundred tokens a second, each on one graphics card.

00:00:05.656 --> 00:00:08.603
So how fast does one person actually read?

00:00:11.123 --> 00:00:12.019
Hi, I'm Tolliver!

00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:15.601
Three posts this week put Qwen three point eight at hundreds of

00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:17.392
tokens a second on one card.

00:00:17.392 --> 00:00:19.183
Each is a sum over streams.

00:00:21.008 --> 00:00:21.764
Chapter one.

00:00:21.764 --> 00:00:23.275
One read, many streams.

00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:31.456
Every decode step streams all the weights from memory into the chip.

00:00:31.456 --> 00:00:35.030
With one user, that read is almost the whole cost, and the

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:36.221
math units sit waiting.

00:00:36.641 --> 00:00:38.560
Put eight streams in the same step.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.205
The weights are still read once.

00:00:40.205 --> 00:00:44.044
Each stream only adds one more row of math, which the card had spare.

00:00:44.464 --> 00:00:48.244
Each stream does bring its own memory: a recurrent state of about

00:00:48.244 --> 00:00:51.080
a hundred and fifty megabytes, plus its KV cache.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.307
One step, eight tokens out.

00:00:53.307 --> 00:00:57.281
The total climbs nearly eightfold, while each user's speed barely moves.

00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:00.111
One stream first.

00:01:00.111 --> 00:01:05.142
Each decode step reads all the weights once: a fixed bar of memory traffic.

00:01:05.142 --> 00:01:06.938
One token's math barely registers.

00:01:07.358 --> 00:01:09.123
Add streams one at a time.

00:01:09.123 --> 00:01:12.947
The weight read never grows; each stream adds only its state and cache.

00:01:12.947 --> 00:01:15.595
The total climbs, and each stream slows a little.

00:01:16.015 --> 00:01:19.104
Past the mid thirties, the math overtakes the memory.

00:01:19.104 --> 00:01:22.879
Every stream now costs its full arithmetic: the total flattens, and

00:01:22.879 --> 00:01:24.251
each stream keeps slowing.

00:01:24.671 --> 00:01:26.121
Now the week's claims.

00:01:26.121 --> 00:01:30.472
HyperQwen's one thousand and thirty-five is sixty-four streams, just under the line.

00:01:30.472 --> 00:01:35.185
The eight-lane runs sit above it: a faster card, and they also draft.

00:01:35.605 --> 00:01:39.539
Seven sixty-four and one thousand and ninety-nine are whole runs.

00:01:39.539 --> 00:01:41.899
Sixteen twenty-three is one best second.

00:01:43.983 --> 00:01:48.031
Here it is measured, by one serving stack on one three-oh-ninety: forty-six

00:01:48.031 --> 00:01:52.079
tokens a second for one stream, three hundred and twenty-one for eight.

00:01:52.499 --> 00:01:55.725
Eight users get seven times the total, and each still sees

00:01:55.725 --> 00:01:56.897
about forty a second.

00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:59.394
Measured, it bends the same way.

00:01:59.394 --> 00:02:04.239
At sixty-four streams the total passes a thousand, and each user gets about sixteen.

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:06.410
Chapter two.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:07.715
What each stream costs.

00:02:12.883 --> 00:02:15.672
Forty-eight of the sixty-four layers are Gated DeltaNet.

00:02:15.672 --> 00:02:20.554
Each keeps a fixed matrix per head, a hundred twenty-eight by a hundred twenty-eight.

00:02:20.974 --> 00:02:24.676
That is about a hundred and fifty megabytes per stream in full

00:02:24.676 --> 00:02:28.686
precision, read and written on every step, before a single token of cache.

00:02:29.106 --> 00:02:33.519
The sixteen attention layers add a KV cache: about thirty-three kilobytes per

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:34.990
token in eight-bit floats.

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:39.498
On the three-oh-ninety, that state capped the batch: only thirty-seven of

00:02:39.498 --> 00:02:40.985
sixty-four requests ever ran.

00:02:41.405 --> 00:02:44.812
Halve the state to sixteen bits, and about seventy short requests fit.

00:02:46.592 --> 00:02:47.458
Chapter three.

00:02:47.458 --> 00:02:48.756
Where speculation fits.

00:02:52.967 --> 00:02:56.791
The five-oh-ninety runs this week split one coding prompt into eight lanes,

00:02:56.791 --> 00:02:59.341
each writing its own part of one file.

00:02:59.761 --> 00:03:03.289
Inside the pass, every lane brings a row for its own next token.

00:03:03.709 --> 00:03:07.189
And each lane brings draft rows: guesses the big model checks in

00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:09.799
the same pass, keeping the ones it agrees with.

00:03:10.219 --> 00:03:11.638
Every row costs arithmetic.

00:03:11.638 --> 00:03:15.896
Batching and drafting draw on the same budget, which is why drafting

00:03:15.896 --> 00:03:19.090
wins below about eight users and batching wins above.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:22.625
Eight lanes with drafts is the sweet spot where both still pay.

00:03:24.425 --> 00:03:29.244
TensorFold's seven hundred is this line: eight parallel code lanes, seven hundred

00:03:29.244 --> 00:03:32.055
sixty-four tokens a second of decode, summed.

00:03:32.475 --> 00:03:35.145
End to end it is six hundred sixty-five.

00:03:35.145 --> 00:03:36.480
Per lane, about ninety-five.

00:03:38.217 --> 00:03:42.638
The sixteen hundred came from Cinference's author, who called it a magic trick.

00:03:42.638 --> 00:03:45.359
The big counter is the fastest single second.

00:03:45.779 --> 00:03:48.643
The run itself averaged one thousand and ninety-nine.

00:03:48.643 --> 00:03:52.939
Like for like, that is one point four times TensorFold, not twice.

00:03:54.175 --> 00:03:56.369
Divide each total by its streams.

00:03:56.369 --> 00:04:00.757
About ninety-five a lane, about a hundred and thirty-seven, and about sixteen

00:04:00.757 --> 00:04:02.585
for each of sixty-four users.

00:04:04.258 --> 00:04:08.293
The project's demo shows its sixty-four at once: short requests, a hundred

00:04:08.293 --> 00:04:10.982
twenty-eight tokens in and five hundred twelve out.

00:04:11.402 --> 00:04:13.720
It is a fine backend for a pipeline.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.617
Its own guide sends chat users to the single-user mode.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:19.025
The totals are real.

00:04:19.025 --> 00:04:20.151
Three things shrink them.

00:04:20.571 --> 00:04:24.577
With eight thousand token prompts, eight streams gave about two point four

00:04:24.577 --> 00:04:26.246
times one stream, not seven.

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:29.870
Lanes finish at different times, so the end of a run

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:31.035
has fewer streams decoding.

00:04:31.455 --> 00:04:33.612
And a fastest second is not an average.

00:04:35.558 --> 00:04:37.287
So ask how many streams first.

00:04:37.287 --> 00:04:41.320
Divide the total by them, and that is the speed a person actually feels.

00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:45.172
Weights are read once per step.

00:04:45.172 --> 00:04:46.861
Each stream adds its own state.

00:04:46.861 --> 00:04:49.394
And to know the speed, divide by the streams.

00:04:49.814 --> 00:04:51.994
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:51.994 --> 00:04:52.616
I'm Tolliver.

00:04:52.616 --> 00:04:52.928
Bye!
